"Marcus" <akf@afdslkj.com> writes:

> I'm not yet certain what is a 'good' number of hp/pp for the endgame. 

Depends on your play style.  I had ascensions with ~150-200hp, but 
I learned that some people find this too low and prefer ~350-400hp.  
For pp, this is entirely up to your char class and playing style.  
Most of my deaths in the endgame, however, are more due to careless 
playing than to too low an initial value of hp/pp.  

> I'm
> also not clear on all the methods of raising each permanently, nor do I know
> how to gain levels past ~16 easily. I just recently tried nurse dancing
> successfully for max hp, but scrolls of genocide aren't common. 

In my experience, nurse dancing doesn't do too much after 150hp.  
Gathering 20 potions of *healing, however, which is not too 
difficult within 30k turns, yields 160hp.  The key is to stuff 
them away until it pays off to alchemize them.  

> I've used
> blessed potions of full healing as well. For raising levels, I know of
> wraiths, but as with nurses, the scrolls are a problem. I know gain energy
> raises max pp, anything else?

Scrolls of charging under some circumstances.  I advocate polypiling 
(if you don't consider it cheating) in order to boost your scroll 
collection.  

> Related to this prep, I hopefully acquire some water walking boots, to make
> scroll/potion blanking easier - is there another method for doing this that
> I'm missing? 

The simplest way is to stash away anything which should not get wet, 
then repeatedly enter a water field.  (Unless you have the teleport 
intrinsic.)  Some care required.  Improvements involve e.g. the usage 
of a certain amulet.  A quite good method would also be to somehow 
de-magic the magic scrolls and potions, if one could find a way 
to do so.  Or, for harmful potions, maybe a means of purifying them 
would be helpful.  

> Also, is there a safe/safer way to acquire disintegration
> resistance? Black Dragons make me *very* nervous, since Reflection isn't
> guaranteed.

Is that so?  I never had any trouble with disintegration rays 
and reflection.  [Maybe you refer to a wide-angle disintegration 
beam emmitted as a consequence of certain theological differences 
between a deity and mortalkind.]  Anyway, since a) monsters tend 
to approach you and b) dragons usually don't use their breath 
weapon in melee with you, there's an obvious tactic to minimize 
breath-related incidents.  

> Similarly, how on earth do you get more magic markers? I tried polypiling a
> ton of tools with no luck, and I rarely find any in a given game. 

That's working as intended.  They are rare and a good wish-candidate.  
If you want higher chances of getting them, try collecting unicorn 
horns; else they are few and scattered.  

> generally wind up burning wishes on them, which is usually just enough to
> fully *proof and enchant my armor and weapons, maybe genocide a species or
> two, and that's about it. More would be very nice.

I would advocate to delay at least the proofing and maybe some 
enchanting until some time later.  [The wishing interlude at the 
castle rapidly boosts your combat value so much that one usually 
can afford to leave some potential untapped, hence I don't think this 
is very dangerous.]  It is quite likely that you will stumble upon 
or be able to polypile quite some scrolls of enchant foo.  

> Are there any means of charging other than scrolls of charging? 

There's a premium way to charge all for free, but it's open 
only to the best of all character classes.  The rest has to 
work with polying and hoping/wishing for scrolls.  Or writing 
them, but that's costly.  

> Once this preparation is complete, I head down into Gehennom (or recently,
> Heck^2 with the patch :)). I absolutely detest this part of the game. I did
> just discover that magic mapping makes this _far_ less annoying, and autodig
> also helps eliminate a ton of the pain. Without magic mapping though... ugh.
> Not fun. Boring, tedious, drawn out. 

That's why I strongly prefer classes capable of a good magic 
mapping spell skill.  

> Even more horrible that the Amulet
> draws this part of the game out even more. 

Once you got the amulet, use your numerous scrolls and wands of 
teleport to reach the upstairs; this saves vast amounts of time 
and consequently Rodney-battles.  

> Polymorphing 0 I've almost never used it in the past. How do you use it?

Make several stashes; don't give too much of the same kind into 
each stash; prefer magic to non-magic gear; economics of scale 
applies (ie, use it on large scale, not for three unicorn horns).  

> Good creatures to Polymorph into? 

Female Grey/Silver Dragons.  Usually saves a wish.  

> Useful intrinsics you can
> acquire by eating rings/amulets? 

Protection.  The rest seems not too useful to me.  

> I always seemed to get into trouble with
> Polymorph, so I rarely use it.

It is much more interesting to poly your pet than yourself.  

> Water - Is praying for Unholy water relatively safe, if I go sac to repair
> diety relationship?

If you consider losing your protection "relatively safe", then yes.  
I prefer to use the confused-remove-curse idea.  

> Summoning Wraiths to gain levels - have not done this, but I'm contemplating
> it now. Recommendations? 

Generally, high level means that generated monsters will be higher 
level.  At level 30, you can meet Archons, and they are a pain.  
OTOH, it boosts magic, but I usually stay at lvl 15-16 and am 
doing fine.  

> them other than genocide? Foocubi - saw this mentioned - again, any
> suggestions on how to utilize this to gain levels?

Tame them.  Or have high enough charisma + intelligence.  

> Pets - favorite pets? Good ways to use them? I tried an Angel, and a Purple
> Worm. I liked the Angel because it didn't eat corpses.

In the early game, a strong pet is nice for cleaning out stores.  
In the later game, a purple worm might be of interest for his 
instakill ability which is useful sometimes.  Remember to speed 
him, and have a magic whistle with you.  

> Riding - never used it - my recent starting Knight died trying to mount his
> pony on level one, which I thought was too stupid for words and quit playing
> them right there ;) Actually useful?

IMHO, more stylish than useful, but I have little experience with it.  

> Inventory - I need to reevaluate this a bit. I usually keep all my extra
> stuff in my bag of holding, but if it gets cursed (or, god forbid, I wield a
> cursed two handed weapon again, shudder), that can be trouble. Without
> reflection, items in my inventory aren't safe. Keep stuff in another sack?

Keeping your bag in a sack is also possible.  

> Near stairs on levels? Speaking of reflection, WITH reflection, what should
> I look out for that can still toast my inventory items?

They might get cursed, but that's mostly it.  Nymphs are more 
dangerous than gnomes with wands of death at this point.  

> Gems - Gem id'ing seems a bit beyond me. Lot of inventory fiddling for
> little to no gain. I know of tossing gems to unicorns, are there any other
> practical uses for gems besides money at shops?

Gems have five reasonable usages I could think of.  Gaining luck, 
gaining money, gaining score, weapon, engraving.  

[voluntary conduct-like behaviour]
> don't price id (feels bizarre). 

That's not the easiest informal conduct.  Without price id, one 
has much more difficulty sorting out things in the early game.  

